were heavily not looking like me.

00:09:45.922 --> 00:09:49.302
It was often very male,
I was kind of the outsider.

00:09:49.302 --> 00:09:52.966
And that had a lot of interesting
implications for how I conducted myself at

00:09:52.966 --> 00:09:56.824
work, and the groups that I was able to
be a part of, and how I would influence.

00:09:56.824 --> 00:10:00.194
And figuring out how to master different
ways of doing that and how to lead a group

00:10:00.194 --> 00:10:03.531
of people who weren't like me, and
kind of that diversity in different ways.

00:10:03.531 --> 00:10:07.816
And how to respond to it myself
are really the reasons for applying.

00:10:07.816 --> 00:10:09.441
&gt;&gt; It's really helpful.

00:10:09.441 --> 00:10:14.642
Thanks, I'm going to go back
to one more slide here.

00:10:14.642 --> 00:10:17.946
And I love the introductions you gave,
because many of you talked about

00:10:17.946 --> 00:10:21.014
this a moment coming to Stanford
to turn inside yourself, and

00:10:21.014 --> 00:10:23.382
actually develop who you are,
know yourself.

00:10:23.382 --> 00:10:28.282
And really that's what leadership
development curriculum is all about at

00:10:28.282 --> 00:10:29.067
Stanford.

00:10:29.067 --> 00:10:33.121
The question we want you to answer is,
why should someone follow you?

00:10:33.121 --> 00:10:37.391
Maybe you're out there in the audience and
you're someone who, whenever you're in

00:10:37.391 --> 00:10:41.083
a room can kind of distill the critical
issues people are speaking about.

00:10:41.083 --> 00:10:44.375
Or maybe you're someone who's
willing to challenge the sacred cow,

00:10:44.375 --> 00:10:45.614
challenge assumptions.

00:10:45.614 --> 00:10:49.254
Or maybe you're the person in the room
who notices that someone else isn't

00:10:49.254 --> 00:10:51.968
participating and
you include them and ask them, hey,

00:10:51.968 --> 00:10:54.359
I'd really love to know
what you're thinking.

00:10:54.359 --> 00:10:56.977
So all these are different
leadership behaviors.

00:10:56.977 --> 00:10:58.928
But we want to help you
answer that question of,

00:10:58.928 --> 00:11:01.089
what are you doing that
you're doing really well?

00:11:01.089 --> 00:11:05.751
But also understand the language of
leadership enough to begin to grow into

00:11:05.751 --> 00:11:07.311
these other behaviors.

00:11:07.311 --> 00:11:12.405
And after studying leadership for 20
years, both in the classroom academically,

00:11:12.405 --> 00:11:16.380
and then also in the private sector,
one thing is very clear to me.

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:19.978
That leadership cannot be learned from
a book, it can't be learned from a case.

00:11:19.978 --> 00:11:22.807
You actually have to go
through the un-comfort and

00:11:22.807 --> 00:11:26.755
the stress of trying on these new
behaviors, and understanding things

00:11:26.755 --> 00:11:30.735
about yourself you haven't seen
before in a really safe environment.

00:11:30.735 --> 00:11:34.497
So we do that in many ways at Stanford,
from the core curriculum and

00:11:34.497 --> 00:11:39.151
leadership labs, to some of our elective
courses that you'll hear about today.

00:11:39.151 --> 00:11:41.880
You can see listed and
even more structured opportunities,

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:45.363
whether they're extracurriculars,
or something like Arbuckle fellows,

00:11:45.363 --> 00:11:46.771
low keynotes or talk program.

00:11:46.771 --> 00:11:50.355
So I'm sure each of the panelists will
give us some of those highlights.

00:11:50.355 --> 00:11:53.507
So with that introduction,
I'll stop sharing, and

00:11:53.507 --> 00:11:56.459
I'm going to now ask
the panelists a few questions.

00:11:56.459 --> 00:12:01.074
I'm going to now ask each of you to start
out, and really, if you can think about

00:12:01.074 --> 00:12:06.186
the one class experience, or even resource
that was most critical in helping you with

00:12:06.186 --> 00:12:11.159
your own leadership development while
you're on campus in the last couple years.

00:12:11.159 --> 00:12:12.601
I'd love to hear that.

00:12:12.601 --> 00:12:14.967
And Abby, maybe I'll start with you.

00:12:14.967 --> 00:12:16.506
We'll go backwards this time.

00:12:18.835 --> 00:12:26.521
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean, there's a lot of
things if I had to pick one one.

00:12:26.521 --> 00:12:31.453
Gosh, I think I would say there was
a class called Paths to Power that I took

00:12:31.453 --> 00:12:34.172
in my second year in the winter quarter.

00:12:34.172 --> 00:12:36.770
And I think that for
a combination of timing reasons and

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:38.134
that you've gone through.

00:12:38.134 --> 00:12:41.942
At the point in which you take an elective
like that in the winter quarter of your

00:12:41.942 --> 00:12:45.460
second year, you've kind of gotten
a little bit of a foundation in terms

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:47.152
of some of the leadership things.

00:12:47.152 --> 00:12:49.765
You've gone through Led labs in
the fall of your first year.

00:12:49.765 --> 00:12:52.812
There's often a lot of other
opportunities you've had up to that,

00:12:52.812 --> 00:12:55.122
including things like,
I was also a fellow, etc.

00:12:55.122 --> 00:12:58.356
But the timing of that I think was really
helpful because it was a really good way

00:12:58.356 --> 00:13:01.402
of consolidating and reframing a lot
of the things I learned in one class.

00:13:01.402 --> 00:13:05.495
And being very pragmatic about how
do you apply some of these things?

00:13:05.495 --> 00:13:08.002
And I think that the biggest takeaway for

00:13:08.002 --> 00:13:12.350
me of it which was bit of a shock to
the system at times but also helpful,

00:13:12.350 --> 00:13:15.984
is that when you're given
a toolkit whatever it might be,

00:13:19.114 --> 00:13:23.426
You have a lot of power with that, right,
of ways in which you deploy it, etc.

00:13:23.426 --> 00:13:27.558
And that gives you the responsibility
to be intentional about it,

00:13:27.558 --> 00:13:32.008
in terms of everything that you've
learned, and the skills you have.

00:13:32.008 --> 00:13:35.422
And the ways in which you apply them, you
have to own whether they ended up being

00:13:35.422 --> 00:13:37.406
kind of the right things or
the wrong things.

00:13:37.406 --> 00:13:41.861
And in the context of things like power,
there are questions that you could ask

00:13:41.861 --> 00:13:45.931
around, like how much do you want to
assert yourself in a room or space?

00:13:45.931 --> 00:13:49.590
And will that result in, how will that
impact a certain relationship, or

00:13:49.590 --> 00:13:51.485
how will that impact a certain thing?

00:13:51.485 --> 00:13:54.040
And there are going to be trade-offs.

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:58.311
And part of that is you have this toolkit
where you get to assess all the different

00:13:58.311 --> 00:14:02.583
options, and you kind of have to be really
intentional about the ways in which you

00:14:02.583 --> 00:14:04.593
go about it and own those decisions.

00:14:04.593 --> 00:14:07.271
And again, you're not going to
be perfect every time, but it's

00:14:07.271 --> 00:14:10.672
about kind of ownership of that in saying
there is no one perfect way of doing it.

00:14:10.672 --> 00:14:13.267
But you have to own it and
take responsibility for

00:14:13.267 --> 00:14:17.520
the ways in which you engage in whatever
that setting and whatever your goal is.

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:20.532
So that was a really good learning,
it sounds corny but

00:14:20.532 --> 00:14:23.621
with great power comes great
responsibility, right?

00:14:23.621 --> 00:14:27.609
When you have all these things that
you're given in that toolkit, it's a lot.

00:14:27.609 --> 00:14:31.344
And figuring out the ways that
you're comfortable with owning that,

00:14:31.344 --> 00:14:33.377
that's kind of the critical pieces.

00:14:33.377 --> 00:14:36.302
You have all these ways that
you can make a decision.

00:14:36.302 --> 00:14:39.756
The thing is that you have to be able
to live with that decision however it

00:14:39.756 --> 00:14:40.747
is that you make it.

00:14:40.747 --> 00:14:42.101
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you, Abby.

00:14:42.101 --> 00:14:44.669
How about you, Sarah?

00:14:44.669 --> 00:14:48.587
&gt;&gt; I think the class that impacted me the
most was called the Lean Launchpad, and

00:14:48.587 --> 00:14:51.675
there's actually a class out
of the School of Engineering.

00:14:51.675 --> 00:14:55.635
But it brings together multidisciplinary
teams across engineering,

00:14:55.635 --> 00:15:00.058
business school, all over the university,
to start a startup in one quarter.

00:15:00.058 --> 00:15:04.418
It is really intense, and it was the first
time it was our winter quarter, so

00:15:04.418 --> 00:15:05.319
we were so new.

00:15:05.319 --> 00:15:08.006
We didn't know anyone and
we had to put together this team.

00:15:08.006 --> 00:15:10.391
And then every single week
basically had to pit it,

00:15:10.391 --> 00:15:12.284
because nothing went
right most of the time.

00:15:12.284 --> 00:15:15.877
[LAUGH] And
I think on key lesson that I take away and

00:15:15.877 --> 00:15:19.395
still practice today is
how to make decisions.

00:15:19.395 --> 00:15:22.010
And when should you make
a decision based on consensus, and

00:15:22.010 --> 00:15:23.800
when is that actually not appropriate.

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:26.850
And for me that was a huge learning
because I was a product manager

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:27.653
before the GSB.

00:15:27.653 --> 00:15:32.220
I didn't have any formal authority until a
lot of my decision-making I thought had to

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:33.436
be done by consensus.

00:15:33.436 --> 00:15:37.136
And so that's how I approached
this in Lean Launchpad, and

00:15:37.136 --> 00:15:40.768
it created a lot of interesting
dynamics within the team.

00:15:40.768 --> 00:15:43.684
There's a lot of places
where it wasn't effective.

00:15:43.684 --> 00:15:48.447
And so one key learning for example is, we
started making decisions by each coming in

00:15:48.447 --> 00:15:52.421
with a really well-formed,
articulated hypothesis and argument.

00:15:52.421 --> 00:15:54.221
And instead of asking people to vote,

00:15:54.221 --> 00:15:58.047
we would have everyone essentially debate
around the table and then ask people,

00:15:58.047 --> 00:16:01.099
what is your conviction on
a level from one to 10?

00:16:01.099 --> 00:16:04.733
And then on some decisions,
we decided, actually,

00:16:04.733 --> 00:16:09.190
this person is the owner and
they're going to own that decision.

00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:12.124
And so how we navigate decisions and how
teams are collected and formed, were huge,

00:16:12.124 --> 00:16:13.381
huge learning through that class.

00:16:13.381 --> 00:16:17.051
That I never would have gotten if,
to your point, a person read a book,

00:16:17.051 --> 00:16:20.580
read a case, seeing it play out
in your life was so powerful.

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:23.803
&gt;&gt; Wow, and that's so cool too because
it's a class that I don't think of is

00:16:23.803 --> 00:16:27.139
typically is part of the main
leadership curriculum but fascinating,

00:16:27.139 --> 00:16:28.040
that's awesome.

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:29.620
How about you, Kerry?

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:33.740
And it can be a class experience,
resource, anything you like.

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:36.689
&gt;&gt; Nothing,
I'll go on the experience part,

00:16:36.689 --> 00:16:41.721
I think I was fortunate enough to serve
the student body as co-president.

00:16:41.721 --> 00:16:45.226
And student government was not
something I had envisioned myself

00:16:45.226 --> 00:16:49.377
doing prior to coming to the GSB, so
it was definitely a stretch experience.

00:16:49.377 --> 00:16:53.688
But it was a perfect opportunity to take
everything we're learning in Lead Labs and

00:16:53.688 --> 00:16:56.275
in all the other leadership
curriculum classes,

00:16:56.275 --> 00:16:59.740
why should people follow you and
put it into practice in real time?

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:02.995
So for instance,
I got to work on my communication skills,

00:17:02.995 --> 00:17:07.490
especially [INAUDIBLE] pandemic where you
have to be talking to people about things

00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:09.264
that no one really knows about.

00:17:09.264 --> 00:17:11.498
So that was something that
was important for me,

00:17:11.498 --> 00:17:15.424
learning how to delegate responsibility,
building camaraderie amongst my peers,

00:17:15.424 --> 00:17:18.603
practicing empathy, especially at
a time when everyone needed it.

00:17:18.603 --> 00:17:20.395
Putting yourself in
the shoes of others and

00:17:20.395 --> 00:17:22.510
understanding how best to support them.

00:17:22.510 --> 00:17:23.101
So that for

00:17:23.101 --> 00:17:27.569
me was definitely the experience that I
know I will be using in 10, 20 years,

00:17:27.569 --> 00:17:32.440
even when you have a crisis, just knowing
how to function during that time.

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:35.004
&gt;&gt; Wow, yeah, and Kerry,
others may not know this but

00:17:35.004 --> 00:17:39.204
you were really the student representative
on the committee that made many decisions

00:17:39.204 --> 00:17:42.782
about how to get through the pandemic
with classes and everything else.

00:17:42.782 --> 00:17:44.504
Was that fair description?

00:17:44.504 --> 00:17:48.549
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it was a wonderful kind
of collaborative experience,

00:17:48.549 --> 00:17:54.325
where I got to become really good friends
with the administrators, the deans, staff.

00:17:54.325 --> 00:17:59.197
Understanding the people behind the scenes
that make the GSB just a magical place it

00:17:59.197 --> 00:18:01.710
is was also a wonderful experience for me.

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:05.773
&gt;&gt; Not an easy job, I'm sure this year,
[LAUGH], how about you,

00:18:05.773 --> 00:18:08.329
Brian, what's most memorable for you?

00:18:10.217 --> 00:18:14.994
&gt;&gt; I was thinking, I also was just typing
in that Kerry's an incredible leader.

00:18:14.994 --> 00:18:16.134
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And we were so

00:18:16.134 --> 00:18:21.971
lucky to have him as one of our
presidents, so plus a million at that.

00:18:21.971 --> 00:18:25.215
I think we're all kind of avoiding talking
about Touchy Feely because that's the big

00:18:25.215 --> 00:18:26.513
one that everyone knows about.

00:18:26.513 --> 00:18:31.471
But the reality is that's a great class
and there are a lot of other classes where

00:18:31.471 --> 00:18:36.429
you get to apply that same experiential
learning cycle where you can say, hey,

00:18:36.429 --> 00:18:38.290
I'm going to make it concrete.

00:18:38.290 --> 00:18:42.271
I think for me, the big realization for
me was that leadership,

00:18:42.271 --> 00:18:44.572
it's not like a personality style.

00:18:44.572 --> 00:18:47.783
It's not like you have one
fixed leadership type,

00:18:47.783 --> 00:18:52.590
it's actually a suite of behaviors and
you can try on different ones.

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:56.195
And some of them may be muscles that are
underdeveloped that you need to practice.

00:18:56.195 --> 00:18:59.998
Some ones, you just may not like using,
like Abby's talking about in the past.

00:18:59.998 --> 00:19:03.902
You may see different approaches and
see the results against them and say, hey,

00:19:03.902 --> 00:19:05.348
I don't like using that one.

00:19:05.348 --> 00:19:06.672
Either because it's uncomfortable or

00:19:06.672 --> 00:19:08.443
maybe you examined why it
makes you uncomfortable.

00:19:08.443 --> 00:19:11.399
And maybe you decide that's just not
going to be one of the ones I'm using.

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:16.631
So the class that actually gave me
the most of those learning cycles was

00:19:16.631 --> 00:19:19.044
the negotiations class, through the loss.

00:19:19.044 --> 00:19:22.939
And so this gets at, there was
a negotiations class in the GSB as well,

00:19:22.939 --> 00:19:26.777
I just happened to do a seminar in
the law school that was full corner.

00:19:26.777 --> 00:19:30.916
And what was really cool about that,
is every single week you had an in-depth

00:19:30.916 --> 00:19:34.877
negotiation where different class
members had to play different roles.

00:19:34.877 --> 00:19:39.764
And you would explicitly reflect
beforehand on what your approach was going

00:19:39.764 --> 00:19:40.686
to be and why.

00:19:40.686 --> 00:19:44.859
And then at the end of the class, even
though you were only in your negotiation,

00:19:44.859 --> 00:19:46.935
everyone would enter in the outcomes.

00:19:46.935 --> 00:19:51.054
And you could see the full range of
different approaches people had taken and

00:19:51.054 --> 00:19:53.060
how it had gone for different folks.

00:19:53.060 --> 00:19:55.835
And you basically realize there's
no right or wrong approach, but

00:19:55.835 --> 00:19:57.977
there are trade-offs that
Abby's talking about,

00:19:57.977 --> 00:20:00.087
and trade-offs that you
have to be willing to own.

00:20:00.087 --> 00:20:03.250
And as you think about going into
negotiations in the future, and

00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:05.101
these aren't just high stakes ones.

00:20:05.101 --> 00:20:08.597
These are any kind of negotiation
about where we're going for dinner?

00:20:08.597 --> 00:20:11.550
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] in the class, but

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:13.519
you think about these things,

00:20:13.519 --> 00:20:18.831
it was a really valuable way to practice
these action impact models that you learn.

00:20:18.831 --> 00:20:23.533
And as an Arbuckle fellow or when you're
choosing okay, what new behaviors am I

00:20:23.533 --> 00:20:27.389
going to try on, how do I expect this
to land on the other person, and

00:20:27.389 --> 00:20:29.446
then in what ways was I surprised?

00:20:29.446 --> 00:20:33.651
And what was different than I expected,
and how do I want to change my behavior

00:20:33.651 --> 00:20:37.811
next time in order to better align my
actual impact with my desired action?

00:20:37.811 --> 00:20:40.551
So that class was the one where
I just got the most reps and

00:20:40.551 --> 00:20:43.170
probably learned the most from doing.

00:20:43.170 --> 00:20:46.905
&gt;&gt; Awesome, and one of the things Stanford
is known for is being able to take coding

00:20:46.905 --> 00:20:50.615
classes across the street because you can
take them in any of the graduate schools.

00:20:50.615 --> 00:20:55.595
So maybe a little, Brian, about was that
hard to find the course, take the course,

00:20:55.595 --> 00:20:57.152
how easy or hard was that?

00:20:57.152 --> 00:20:58.405
&gt;&gt; It's super easy,

00:20:58.405 --> 00:21:03.419
that class was one where just I've been
talking to other classmates about,

00:21:03.419 --> 00:21:08.211
hey, if I want to get some reps doing,
I'm uncomfortable negotiating.

00:21:08.211 --> 00:21:11.188
I actually think it was Sarah, [LAUGH]
who was the one who recommended it to me.

00:21:11.188 --> 00:21:13.917
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My gosh, this one is great, and

00:21:13.917 --> 00:21:17.577
I remember kind of geeking out with
her at dinner one night hearing about

00:21:17.577 --> 00:21:21.377
a negotiation she was preparing for,
and I just took it the next quarter.

00:21:21.377 --> 00:21:25.881
But it's really easy to do those, I think
folks will do classes in med school and

00:21:25.881 --> 00:21:30.251
a lot of us take the computer science
class just because you're at Stanford,

00:21:30.251 --> 00:21:32.693
you can try something totally different.

00:21:32.693 --> 00:21:35.430
So it's really easy to do that and
it's a great complement.

00:21:35.430 --> 00:21:37.280
&gt;&gt; Awesome, that's great, and Francisca.

00:21:38.310 --> 00:21:42.742
&gt;&gt; Sure, I think the one
that really crystallized for

00:21:42.742 --> 00:21:49.867
me was almost the very first day at the
GSB, you do this desert survival exercise.

00:21:49.867 --> 00:21:54.030
Where, basically, you're put in
a room with fellow peers, and

00:21:54.030 --> 00:21:56.754
you're told that you
landed in a desert and

00:21:56.754 --> 00:22:01.315
you have to choose and prioritize
a list of stuff that you take with you.

00:22:01.315 --> 00:22:04.831
And I'm not going to tell
you what the right list is,

00:22:04.831 --> 00:22:09.626
but it's a surprising array of
things that you should prioritize.

00:22:09.626 --> 00:22:12.258
And afterwards, you're recorded and

00:22:12.258 --> 00:22:16.973
you watch the video of yourself doing
it and you kind of talk through it.

00:22:16.973 --> 00:22:22.919
And the group that you're with is your
Leadership Labs group that you're with for

00:22:22.919 --> 00:22:24.393
an entire quarter.

00:22:24.393 --> 00:22:29.104
And I guess that even just
in the first week allowed me

00:22:29.104 --> 00:22:33.601
to kind of better reflect
on who I am in groups, and

00:22:33.601 --> 00:22:37.473
who I want to be and
what are the gaps there.

00:22:37.473 --> 00:22:41.641
And then later on, I decided to apply to
be an Arbuckle fellow which I guess we'll

00:22:41.641 --> 00:22:42.635
talk about later.

00:22:42.635 --> 00:22:47.419
But then I got to leave groups through
that exercise and think about, okay,

00:22:47.419 --> 00:22:52.349
you can't change essentially who you are,
but you can try to see what can I do to

00:22:52.349 --> 00:22:56.430
enhance how I show up in groups and
how I show up as a leader?

00:22:56.430 --> 00:22:59.953
And I think it was just right away,
it hit me in the face, what,

00:22:59.953 --> 00:23:01.960
it was going to be a learning journey.

00:23:03.230 --> 00:23:05.518
&gt;&gt; Francisca,
would you mind sharing something,

00:23:05.518 --> 00:23:08.940
because that was a wonderful example,
to make it concrete for the audience?

00:23:08.940 --> 00:23:13.256
Is there something you learned about
how you showed up that you put into or

00:23:13.256 --> 00:23:15.253
you worked on or developed more?

00:23:15.253 --> 00:23:16.616
Or is that too personal I want to be,

00:23:16.616 --> 00:23:21.248
[CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'm happy to, so my group was

00:23:21.248 --> 00:23:27.514
right away one of the guys came in,
military background.

00:23:27.514 --> 00:23:31.698
He's like, I got this, [LAUGH] racks,
knuckles, gets to work and

00:23:31.698 --> 00:23:34.515
he was totally wrong about everything-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:23:34.515 --> 00:23:37.205
&gt;&gt; Which he was really apologetic for

00:23:37.205 --> 00:23:41.656
afterwards, but
I realized throughout the whole time,

00:23:41.656 --> 00:23:46.122
I was, hey guys,
our time is tight we gotta keep moving.

00:23:46.122 --> 00:23:47.879
How about we do this,
how about we do this?

00:23:47.879 --> 00:23:52.787
And it crystallized that I'm
really process oriented, And

00:23:52.787 --> 00:23:58.243
that is a very real skill that
some people will have less of.

00:23:58.243 --> 00:24:03.972
And so it helps to put words to what
kind of thinker and presence I have.

00:24:03.972 --> 00:24:07.744
And then also how to counteract what,
there's a big ideas people or

00:24:07.744 --> 00:24:11.533
there's people who stay quiet and
how to bring all those things in.

00:24:11.533 --> 00:24:15.068
So that was my kind of
journey was realizing that,

00:24:15.068 --> 00:24:19.966
and then kind of focusing on that
later and enhancing that as a skill.

00:24:19.966 --> 00:24:20.982
&gt;&gt; Awesome, great.

00:24:20.982 --> 00:24:24.211
We had one question come in from the chat,

00:24:24.211 --> 00:24:28.745
I'd love to just ask you,
I think it's a great question.

00:24:28.745 --> 00:24:33.306
So for anyone that we met, can you
describe a time at the GSB when you were

00:24:33.306 --> 00:24:35.738
pushed way out of your comfort zone but

00:24:35.738 --> 00:24:39.480
ultimately grew in your
leadership skills because of it?

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:41.975
So we got a little taste
of that from Francisca.

00:24:41.975 --> 00:24:44.325
Anyone else want to take that on?

00:24:44.325 --> 00:24:48.312
And all of you have had
incredible leadership development

00:24:48.312 --> 00:24:50.191
opportunities on campus.

00:24:50.191 --> 00:24:51.160
Anybody want to answer that?

00:24:55.563 --> 00:24:58.309
&gt;&gt; I can go ahead and-
&gt;&gt; Great, have it, Love.

00:24:58.309 --> 00:25:02.795
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so in your first quarter in
Stanford, you take a class called

00:25:02.795 --> 00:25:07.519
lead labs, which is the class Francisca
has talked a little bit about,

00:25:07.519 --> 00:25:09.662
the desert survival activity.

00:25:09.662 --> 00:25:14.774
And actually around week, I believe
it's week five or six of the curriculum,

00:25:14.774 --> 00:25:18.970
you do something, which is feedback
to everyone in your squad.

00:25:18.970 --> 00:25:22.631
So you have a squad, I think was
mentioned, of six people or so, and then

00:25:22.631 --> 00:25:27.650
an Arbuckle leadership fellow, which is a
second year, who helps kind of facilitate.

00:25:27.650 --> 00:25:29.875
And in that feedback session,

00:25:29.875 --> 00:25:35.621
you kind of talk about like who makes you
feel heard, and who makes you feel seen.

00:25:35.621 --> 00:25:41.332
And I had a member of my squad, who, it's
not that anything had actually happened,

00:25:41.332 --> 00:25:44.724
there was no real conflict or
anything like that.

00:25:44.724 --> 00:25:49.456
But I said to him, I feel like sometimes
you dismiss me and that you don't like me,

00:25:49.456 --> 00:25:51.321
and I don't know that's true.

00:25:51.321 --> 00:25:55.080
But that's something that I've
internalized, and I don't know why?

00:25:55.080 --> 00:25:58.044
And I don't know if that's true,
but it's something that I feel,

00:25:58.044 --> 00:25:59.968
which was a really scary
thing to say aloud.

00:25:59.968 --> 00:26:02.173
Because again, like I said,
there was no real conflict.

00:26:02.173 --> 00:26:05.614
It was just the sense of that,
like there was something there and

00:26:05.614 --> 00:26:08.496
I couldn't really explain
why it existed but it did.

00:26:08.496 --> 00:26:12.422
And it turned into the probably still the
single most productive conversation I've

00:26:12.422 --> 00:26:13.140
had at the GSB,

00:26:13.140 --> 00:26:16.415
which was this person who is from
a very different background from me.

00:26:16.415 --> 00:26:20.352
Looks very different from me,
has very different kind of interests,

00:26:20.352 --> 00:26:21.363
passions, etc.

00:26:21.363 --> 00:26:26.440
Looking at me and saying, you're right,
I've kind of felt the same thing too.

00:26:26.440 --> 00:26:29.695
And again, they're not being a major
fight, they're not even had been somebody

00:26:29.695 --> 00:26:32.827
saying, you said something to me that
I didn't like, there was none of that.

00:26:32.827 --> 00:26:37.984
It was just, I've had this impression of
you since you walked in, you're a blond

00:26:37.984 --> 00:26:42.419
haired, blue eyed, big personality,
kind of likes to fill up a room.

00:26:42.419 --> 00:26:45.477
And I just figured that we would
never be friends and that you and

00:26:45.477 --> 00:26:48.027
I would never really actively
spend time together.

00:26:48.027 --> 00:26:52.081
And that you were going to just occupy
a completely different space than I was.

00:26:52.081 --> 00:26:56.489
And so I decided not to prioritize
getting to know you more, etc.

00:26:56.489 --> 00:26:59.670
And that started a whole conversation,
right,

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:03.946
around impressions that we had of
each other and what that meant.

00:27:03.946 --> 00:27:07.452
And it got us to a really powerful place,
I would say, of friendship and

00:27:07.452 --> 00:27:11.427
he's still someone that I'm actually
probably very, very close to at the GSB,

00:27:11.427 --> 00:27:14.536
who checks me on a lot of things and
I check him on a lot of things.

00:27:14.536 --> 00:27:17.990
But I think it drove a couple of lessons
for me that were really important.

00:27:17.990 --> 00:27:22.721
I think the first was that, weirdly,
especially when you're talking about soft

00:27:22.721 --> 00:27:27.042
skills or kind of these other core
competencies that we don't talk about,

00:27:27.042 --> 00:27:30.007
where you can't name
something in a spreadsheet.

00:27:30.007 --> 00:27:33.986
Often, what you're feeling are your
instincts, even though you can't name

00:27:33.986 --> 00:27:37.921
them and might be like it's not this
thing that I can explain logically.

00:27:37.921 --> 00:27:41.847
That doesn't make it any less real or
any less present at that time.

00:27:41.847 --> 00:27:45.796
And it's something that I think,
as a leader, has to be acknowledged,

00:27:45.796 --> 00:27:47.561
even if you can't quantify it.

00:27:47.561 --> 00:27:52.235
I think, two, it really taught me how to
start thinking about the conversations

00:27:52.235 --> 00:27:56.789
that are really difficult and scary to
have around what is going on between us?

00:27:56.789 --> 00:27:58.609
And talking about naming that thing and

00:27:58.609 --> 00:28:01.266
then having a really difficult
conversation around it.

00:28:01.266 --> 00:28:05.460
That's ultimately very productive and it's
led to some very close friendships, but

00:28:05.460 --> 00:28:08.053
again, it's really scary
thing to do in the moment.

00:28:08.053 --> 00:28:12.553
And then finally, it really kind of hit
home for me a couple of things that I

00:28:12.553 --> 00:28:16.545
need to recognize about myself in
any situation that I show up in,

00:28:16.545 --> 00:28:19.255
which is that I show up
in a very certain way.

00:28:19.255 --> 00:28:22.659
I am somebody who can be pretty loud and
is willing to speak up first.

00:28:22.659 --> 00:28:26.332
And I look a certain way, and I dress
a certain way, and I act a certain way.

00:28:26.332 --> 00:28:28.938
And that can be very approachable and
very warm and

00:28:28.938 --> 00:28:30.999
friendly to certain groups of people.

00:28:30.999 --> 00:28:34.377
And to others, it might be very
alienating and understanding, okay,

00:28:34.377 --> 00:28:38.061
what are the situations in which this
might be alienating and how do I want it?

00:28:38.061 --> 00:28:41.779
It comes back to kind of owning the
decisions you make of I like to show up

00:28:41.779 --> 00:28:44.438
this way,
it feels like an expression of myself.

00:28:44.438 --> 00:28:47.369
But then for someone to whom
it doesn't resonate with them,

00:28:47.369 --> 00:28:48.928
how do I want to engage with that?

00:28:48.928 --> 00:28:50.315
And how do I kind of show up for them?

00:28:50.315 --> 00:28:54.185
And how do I approach them and
thinking about with every kind of, again,

00:28:54.185 --> 00:28:57.167
every choice you make,
you are bringing people in, but

00:28:57.167 --> 00:28:59.664
you also might be kind
of throwing people off.

00:28:59.664 --> 00:29:02.004
And so
thinking about that very deliberately and

00:29:02.004 --> 00:29:06.126
knowing that something that happens, even
when it is just showing up in a classroom.

00:29:06.126 --> 00:29:08.459
And being like, hi, good morning and
being very excited and

00:29:08.459 --> 00:29:10.080
knowing that's not everyone's style.

00:29:10.080 --> 00:29:14.198
And so getting a sense of that and knowing
that that's choices that you can make, and

00:29:14.198 --> 00:29:17.910
that you can kind of lean into I think
was really powerful for me to learn, and

00:29:17.910 --> 00:29:19.085
it started very early.

00:29:19.085 --> 00:29:20.628
&gt;&gt; Wow, thank you, Abby, for sharing that.

00:29:20.628 --> 00:29:22.004
All right, Anna,

00:29:22.004 --> 00:29:27.086
you had a question that was very similar
to another one I was going to ask.

00:29:27.086 --> 00:29:32.082
Basically, any thoughts or insights on how
you thought about leadership before coming

00:29:32.082 --> 00:29:33.374
to the GSB versus now?

00:29:33.374 --> 00:29:36.707
So similar to where Abby was going, but

00:29:36.707 --> 00:29:40.956
some big learning aha,
and how did you get there?

00:29:40.956 --> 00:29:43.163
So anyone can go next.

00:29:43.163 --> 00:29:43.921
How about you, Brian?

00:29:43.921 --> 00:29:46.571
Yeah, thanks.
&gt;&gt; Yeah, this was a big aha for me.

00:29:46.571 --> 00:29:51.417
I had applied for the Arbuckle
programme because of this curriculum

00:29:51.417 --> 00:29:55.725
on teaching about leadership
coaching in particular.

00:29:55.725 --> 00:30:00.836
And I think when I had applied to that,
I equated leadership coaching with

00:30:00.836 --> 00:30:05.211
managing or mentoring, and one of the-
&gt;&gt; Can I jump in for a second, Brian?

00:30:05.211 --> 00:30:06.036
Will you explain,

00:30:06.036 --> 00:30:09.398
I don't know that everyone in the audience
knows what an Arbuckle fellow is.

00:30:09.398 --> 00:30:12.411
So it'd be great for
you to get the top level too, it's great

00:30:12.411 --> 00:30:13.031
&gt;&gt; Sure, so,

00:30:13.031 --> 00:30:16.328
high level, we talked a lot about
this leadership labs class.

00:30:16.328 --> 00:30:19.875
So that is a first quarter
class that every MBA one takes.

00:30:19.875 --> 00:30:24.920
And each of those squads of six
people is led by one second year MBA.

00:30:24.920 --> 00:30:29.839
And they're not the teacher in that class,
they're kind of a facilitator.

00:30:29.839 --> 00:30:35.162
And in order to facilitate that, those
second years are called Arbuckle fellows,

00:30:35.162 --> 00:30:37.684
and you have to apply for this program.

00:30:37.684 --> 00:30:42.244
And if you are selected as an Arbuckle
fellow, you take touchy freely, your

00:30:42.244 --> 00:30:47.031
spring quarter of your first year, so you
tend to get to take a little bit early.

00:30:47.031 --> 00:30:50.825
And then, your second year,
you're doing three different things,

00:30:50.825 --> 00:30:54.764
kind of three parallel tracks that
are part of leadership development.

00:30:54.764 --> 00:30:58.157
The first is you're in a class
called leadership coaching,

00:30:58.157 --> 00:31:02.747
which is a two-quarter long class, where
you are having regular weekly sessions

00:31:02.747 --> 00:31:06.619
with readings on academic content
related to leadership coaching.

00:31:06.619 --> 00:31:10.483
And in parallel to that,
you're also having a hands-on practical,

00:31:10.483 --> 00:31:13.365
which from the leadership
fellow's perspective,

00:31:13.365 --> 00:31:16.192
means you are learning by
trying to lead the squad.

00:31:16.192 --> 00:31:19.310
And I think from the first
year's perspective, I thought,

00:31:19.310 --> 00:31:23.354
the second year really knows what they're
doing, and I really admired my leaders.

00:31:23.354 --> 00:31:27.031
From the secondaries perspective,
I have no idea what I'm doing,

00:31:27.031 --> 00:31:29.558
but I learned a lot because
I messing up [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:29.558 --> 00:31:33.830
But it actually is a very hands-on way
of learning about leadership coaching

00:31:33.830 --> 00:31:34.430
by doing.

00:31:34.430 --> 00:31:40.080
And so track one is content,
track two is practicum.

00:31:40.080 --> 00:31:43.337
The third is about
a community where you have a,

00:31:43.337 --> 00:31:47.257
it's not called a squad, but
it functions like a squad.

00:31:47.257 --> 00:31:51.510
Or it's five other Arbuckle fellows who
meet us weekly and you're sharing about

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:55.940
your learning challenges, and
getting support and one on one coaching.

00:31:55.940 --> 00:31:57.629
Over the course of this curriculum.

00:31:57.629 --> 00:32:01.084
So it is a pretty rich three quarter
long starting in the spring of your

00:32:01.084 --> 00:32:04.422
first year and continuing through
the winter of your second year,

00:32:04.422 --> 00:32:08.832
where you're getting classroom learning,
hands on learning, and peer support, and

00:32:08.832 --> 00:32:12.002
is a longer answer, but does that-
&gt;&gt; That's helpful, yeah.

00:32:12.002 --> 00:32:14.494
Thanks, Brian, so your learning was, yeah.

00:32:14.494 --> 00:32:15.682
&gt;&gt; Okay, so my learning was,

00:32:15.682 --> 00:32:19.100
leadership coaching is totally
different from managing or mentoring.

00:32:19.100 --> 00:32:23.467
And a lot of times the ladder, this
managing mentoring is about optimizing

00:32:23.467 --> 00:32:27.224
performance, helping people
achieve their short term goals.

00:32:27.224 --> 00:32:31.860
And coaching is much less about achieving
the short term performance goals,

00:32:31.860 --> 00:32:36.950
as helping the person that your coaching
discover the answers for themselves.

00:32:36.950 --> 00:32:38.171
And fundamentally,

00:32:38.171 --> 00:32:42.588
it's about expanding their sense of
the choices that are available to them.

00:32:42.588 --> 00:32:46.607
Helping them decide which choices
are most appealing to them, and

00:32:46.607 --> 00:32:51.460
helping them build confidence in their
ability to pursue those choices.

00:32:51.460 --> 00:32:55.845
And it's really about not telling them
what's best, not giving them advice,

00:32:55.845 --> 00:32:58.890
not even being anxious to
reach the right answer.

00:32:58.890 --> 00:33:02.337
It's about helping them
explore the landscape, and

00:33:02.337 --> 00:33:06.395
helping them grow in self awareness,
and helping them grow and

00:33:06.395 --> 00:33:09.706
feeling capable of trying
on these new behaviors.

00:33:09.706 --> 00:33:13.802
And a lot of that, I guess to go back to
that earlier question about going way

00:33:13.802 --> 00:33:17.702
outside your comfort zone, a lot of
the things that a coach can do is help

00:33:17.702 --> 00:33:22.000
identify areas that are just a little
bit outside of their comfort zone.

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:26.877
But that might then let them try a new
behavior that's not super scary, but

00:33:26.877 --> 00:33:29.140
isn't that 15% stretch zone.

00:33:29.140 --> 00:33:32.563
And those are the ways that
a coach can really help, and

00:33:32.563 --> 00:33:36.350
it's super different from
being a mentor or a manager.

00:33:36.350 --> 00:33:40.570
And so that was a real
expansion of mindset for me.

00:33:40.570 --> 00:33:41.218
&gt;&gt; That's cool.

00:33:41.218 --> 00:33:42.670
Thank you, Brian.

00:33:42.670 --> 00:33:45.030
We've gotten a couple questions
already from the chat.

00:33:45.030 --> 00:33:49.210
I want to make sure, Sarah,
you're a talk coach,

00:33:49.210 --> 00:33:52.402
we haven't talked at all about that.

00:33:52.402 --> 00:33:54.470
I think that's such a cool part
of our leadership development.

00:33:54.470 --> 00:33:57.104
Would you mind maybe talking
about its influence on you, and

00:33:57.104 --> 00:33:58.146
how you became a coach?

00:33:58.146 --> 00:34:02.478
&gt;&gt; Sure, so Tucker, I briefly mentioned
it but it's essentially this tradition,

00:34:02.478 --> 00:34:06.306
where every single week two members of
the class get up either in person or

00:34:06.306 --> 00:34:09.910
later virtually and
share their life in 30 minutes.

00:34:09.910 --> 00:34:14.240
So it's this incredible tradition
because 30 minutes is a long time.

00:34:14.240 --> 00:34:18.706
And people are so open and vulnerable and
generous with this community, and

00:34:18.706 --> 00:34:21.306
really opening up and
sharing who they are.

00:34:21.306 --> 00:34:25.045
And it's not professional,
it is deeply personal.

00:34:25.045 --> 00:34:26.134
For me as a coach,

00:34:26.134 --> 00:34:30.362
I wanted to do it ironically because
I did not want to give my own talk.

00:34:30.362 --> 00:34:33.266
But I loved the idea of talking,
I was like, I'll let others go.

00:34:33.266 --> 00:34:37.590
I'll support them, and I did not realize
that talk coaches have to give a talk.

00:34:37.590 --> 00:34:41.880
And so I got that out of the way pretty
early, and giving a talk itself was really

00:34:41.880 --> 00:34:46.301
transformational, when else you have
the opportunity to re examine your life,

00:34:46.301 --> 00:34:48.912
make sense of it, and
then share it with a room.

00:34:48.912 --> 00:34:53.507
And I think being a coach for me, I think
to Brian's earlier point, it's not,

00:34:53.507 --> 00:34:58.259
I used to be so focused on, okay, there's
output that we're all creating here.

00:34:58.259 --> 00:35:01.426
There's a 30 minute talk and I'm going to
get you there, and here are the steps.

00:35:01.426 --> 00:35:05.645
And I sort of halfway through the fall
quarter, stepped back and said,

00:35:05.645 --> 00:35:10.661
it's about allowing this person to have
space to answer questions for themselves.

00:35:10.661 --> 00:35:12.910
And I can be a mirror back to them.

00:35:12.910 --> 00:35:16.774
And so I think about that for coaching
broadly and being really a mirror and

00:35:16.774 --> 00:35:21.073
someone to help ask questions and guide,
but ultimately, it's their process,

00:35:21.073 --> 00:35:22.530
it's their output.

00:35:22.530 --> 00:35:26.661
And I am just always blown away by
the talks we hear every single week,

00:35:26.661 --> 00:35:31.449
even if I've heard it five times, I'm
still in tears [LAUGH] every single week.

00:35:31.449 --> 00:35:32.620
It's been really special for me.

00:35:33.710 --> 00:35:36.700
&gt;&gt; Awesome and just to keep you on
the hotspot, Sarah for a minute longer.

00:35:36.700 --> 00:35:38.436
One of the things I
love about Stanford is,

00:35:38.436 --> 00:35:41.970
we think about leadership as also building
your brand and being able to communicate.

00:35:41.970 --> 00:35:45.336
So top is part of that knowing
what you value, who you are,

00:35:45.336 --> 00:35:49.696
you also did low keynote and you were
a TA for winning writing, I remember.

00:35:49.696 --> 00:35:51.941
So you've had a whole
brand building experience,

00:35:51.941 --> 00:35:54.510
any highlights on those
two things as well?

00:35:54.510 --> 00:35:55.842
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thanks for bringing those up.

00:35:55.842 --> 00:35:59.280
So on the keynote in particular,
that was exactly why I did it.

00:35:59.280 --> 00:36:03.190
So low keynote, for those who don't know
is sort of that TED style talk that you

00:36:03.190 --> 00:36:06.225
can give, and you do at the big
stage at Siemens Auditorium.

00:36:06.225 --> 00:36:08.770
It's really high production
value of things.

00:36:08.770 --> 00:36:10.290
We just going to [LAUGH] and

00:36:10.290 --> 00:36:14.135
I did it on a topic that is
really near and dear to my heart.

00:36:14.135 --> 00:36:17.736
It's how we should re-imagine aging and
the process of growing older.

00:36:17.736 --> 00:36:20.555
And also really strategic because
the startup I'm working on is

00:36:20.555 --> 00:36:21.450
in the aging space.

00:36:21.450 --> 00:36:25.574
And so I wanted to have a piece of content
out there that was really professional,

00:36:25.574 --> 00:36:26.968
that I was really proud of,

00:36:26.968 --> 00:36:30.460
that I could put it on my LinkedIn,
and send to investors.

00:36:30.460 --> 00:36:35.115
And also, I don't think many people know
this, but I actually do not like public

00:36:35.115 --> 00:36:38.712
speaking, I have kind of a around it
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:36:38.712 --> 00:36:41.144
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it was also an opportunity to

00:36:41.144 --> 00:36:46.010
push myself way outside my comfort
zone and deliver a nine minute really

00:36:46.010 --> 00:36:50.080
rehearsed Professional Speech,
and it's been awesome.

00:36:50.080 --> 00:36:54.127
For winning writing, I love writing,
I actually, and Abby was also TA for

00:36:54.127 --> 00:36:54.843
that class.

00:36:54.843 --> 00:36:55.562
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:36:55.562 --> 00:36:58.912
&gt;&gt; Glenn Kramon, if you have
a chance to take a class with him,

00:36:58.912 --> 00:37:02.185
I would highly recommend,
he is just the best.

00:37:02.185 --> 00:37:06.918
And so for me it was more about getting
the opportunity to spend time with Glenn.

00:37:06.918 --> 00:37:09.487
That really drew me into that opportunity.

00:37:09.487 --> 00:37:13.814
&gt;&gt; Awesome, and the output of that class,
I always find fascinating, right?

00:37:13.814 --> 00:37:15.706
Because you're writing opinion pieces,
is that right?

00:37:15.706 --> 00:37:18.560
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so the capstone piece
is that you write an OP ED, and

00:37:18.560 --> 00:37:22.123
Glenn is a reporter at the New York times,
and editor in New York times.

00:37:22.123 --> 00:37:25.378
And he will go above and
beyond to get you published.

00:37:25.378 --> 00:37:30.487
He has an outrageous email chains, trying
to get you published every which way,

00:37:30.487 --> 00:37:33.654
and he will go lay out over
his skis to do it for you.

00:37:33.654 --> 00:37:37.821
He's amazing, and we'll advocate for
his students literally for

00:37:37.821 --> 00:37:39.731
decades after they graduate.

00:37:39.731 --> 00:37:41.018
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you.

00:37:41.018 --> 00:37:43.849
I'm going to start switching now
to the questions in the chat.

00:37:43.849 --> 00:37:47.256
So Michael asked one, and ten others
that they'd love to hear the answer.

00:37:47.256 --> 00:37:50.670
What causes Professor
situations from the GSPD,

00:37:50.670 --> 00:37:53.683
you believe you will go back and
reference?

00:37:53.683 --> 00:37:57.652
The most should be people and
companies going for it.

00:38:02.168 --> 00:38:04.360
Raise your hand if that resonates.

00:38:04.360 --> 00:38:06.414
And all of you are working.

00:38:06.414 --> 00:38:09.696
So [LAUGH] this should be
becoming really real to you.

00:38:15.586 --> 00:38:16.566
Anyone?

00:38:16.566 --> 00:38:18.681
I'm sorry, Carrie,
I didn't see your hand, Carrie, awesome.

00:38:18.681 --> 00:38:19.623
&gt;&gt; I can help in here.

00:38:19.623 --> 00:38:22.637
So one that I've actually
had to go back and

00:38:22.637 --> 00:38:27.346
reference it's called strategic
Pivoting with Allison Kluger.

00:38:27.346 --> 00:38:31.797
And it also has Alex Rodriguez,
the baseball legend,

00:38:31.797 --> 00:38:34.911
as one of the professors at the class.

00:38:34.911 --> 00:38:39.433
And I think what's really important about
the class is similar to winning-writing.

00:38:39.433 --> 00:38:41.790
It's about understanding your audience.

00:38:41.790 --> 00:38:43.151
And if you want to be a leader,

00:38:43.151 --> 00:38:46.660
any leader knows that you have to
know the people you're leading.

00:38:46.660 --> 00:38:48.981
And I think every time I have to do stuff,

00:38:48.981 --> 00:38:52.067
I'm currently on a short
term internship in sports.

00:38:52.067 --> 00:38:56.812
And understanding exactly who I'm speaking
to and understanding how to get buy-in and

00:38:56.812 --> 00:39:01.252
exactly how I went to sell myself, because
we all contain multitudes as leaders.

00:39:01.252 --> 00:39:05.119
And your different parts of yourself you
want to bring as you're trying to actually

00:39:05.119 --> 00:39:07.120
get buy-in regarding different things.

00:39:07.120 --> 00:39:10.349
So sometimes I'll go back and
look at the strengths I've identified, and

00:39:10.349 --> 00:39:13.475
figure out which things I want to put
at the fore of whatever I'm trying to

00:39:13.475 --> 00:39:15.540
bring during a meeting,
and things like that.

00:39:15.540 --> 00:39:18.456
And that's actually been very
helpful this summer already.

00:39:18.456 --> 00:39:19.230
&gt;&gt; Wow, that's great.

00:39:19.230 --> 00:39:24.536
And Allison's another example,
she was a TV reporter, is that right?

00:39:24.536 --> 00:39:25.180
Am I getting that right?

00:39:25.180 --> 00:39:28.491
So you'll see at Stanford that
in your second year electives,

00:39:28.491 --> 00:39:32.244
they're often co-taught by a tenured
faculty member in a lecture,.

00:39:32.244 --> 00:39:35.630
Sometimes their individual courses like
Allison's teaching, or Glenn's teaching.

00:39:35.630 --> 00:39:39.178
But we'd like to bring practitioners in,
as well as the academics so

00:39:39.178 --> 00:39:42.558
you're exposed to the art and
the science of how you need to lead.

00:39:42.558 --> 00:39:43.214
That's great, thank you.

00:39:43.214 --> 00:39:48.613
Let's go to another one, what has
changed about, and this is from Saya.

00:39:48.613 --> 00:39:50.688
I hope I saying it right, Sayale.

00:39:50.688 --> 00:39:54.543
What has changed about your leadership
style due to your experiences and

00:39:54.543 --> 00:39:55.688
learning at the GSB?

00:39:55.688 --> 00:39:58.740
Now, a couple of you,
Abby already had given us an example.

00:39:58.740 --> 00:39:59.730
Francisca, tell us.

00:40:00.820 --> 00:40:04.616
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I worked in international
development before, and I had to,

00:40:04.616 --> 00:40:08.606
because I was a young woman, I had to
present a really professional image,

00:40:08.606 --> 00:40:10.147
which I think is important.

00:40:10.147 --> 00:40:13.294
But I kind of came in
thinking I had two hats.

00:40:13.294 --> 00:40:17.168
I had personal Francisca and
then I had professional Francisca, and

00:40:17.168 --> 00:40:19.454
they were kind of two different things.

00:40:19.454 --> 00:40:22.810
There were some similarities,
but not really.

00:40:22.810 --> 00:40:28.383
And I realized how much the GSB, and
some people come in just knowing this,

00:40:28.383 --> 00:40:31.848
they're just themselves always, I was not.

00:40:31.848 --> 00:40:36.921
That collapsing those two ideas and
kind of showing yourself fully

00:40:36.921 --> 00:40:43.430
to different teams and showing up truly
as yourself and sharing of yourself.

00:40:43.430 --> 00:40:49.058
I know this is entirely the stereotype
of GSB, that it's very touchy-feely,

00:40:49.058 --> 00:40:53.683
but it is exactly what I learned,
which is when you show up fully.

00:40:53.683 --> 00:40:58.108
And that doesn't mean revealing all of
your secrets all at once or whatever, but

00:40:58.108 --> 00:41:01.772
there's a power to it,
because it models what is okay for others.

00:41:01.772 --> 00:41:05.739
And as a leader, what you show and
what you share and

00:41:05.739 --> 00:41:09.690
how you share it,
it kind of sets the tone.

00:41:09.690 --> 00:41:12.610
And I saw that over and over again,

00:41:12.610 --> 00:41:17.750
I was part of the Hispanic
Business Student Association, or

00:41:17.750 --> 00:41:22.914
even just in interactions with peers,
there was just a lot.

00:41:22.914 --> 00:41:27.387
When you kind of share your inner thoughts
a bit more and let people in on kind of

00:41:27.387 --> 00:41:31.161
how you're thinking about things,
there's a power to that.

00:41:31.161 --> 00:41:36.094
And that was definitely a change
from kind of the professional

00:41:36.094 --> 00:41:38.809
facade that I had before the GSB.

00:41:38.809 --> 00:41:41.371
&gt;&gt; Francisca,
I find myself just nodding and nodding and

00:41:41.371 --> 00:41:43.999
nodding after being a facilitator and
touchy-feely.

00:41:43.999 --> 00:41:47.380
I think that's amazing response.

00:41:47.380 --> 00:41:50.768
One of the things I always say is we
meet students on your admit weekend,

00:41:50.768 --> 00:41:52.415
so I get a chance to meet all of you.

00:41:52.415 --> 00:41:55.692
And then two years later as you
walk across that graduation stage,

00:41:55.692 --> 00:41:59.789
there's this sense of comfort in your own
skin, and almost feels like some of these

00:41:59.789 --> 00:42:03.890
masks have come away, I know, and
touchy-feely, that's how it felt.

00:42:03.890 --> 00:42:08.088
But anybody else who took that
class want to speak to this,

00:42:08.088 --> 00:42:12.983
because I think it's such a critical
issue of how you learn to do that for

00:42:12.983 --> 00:42:15.190
yourself through the class?

00:42:15.190 --> 00:42:17.281
Did anyone else take touchy-feely?

00:42:17.281 --> 00:42:18.274
Well, yeah.

00:42:18.274 --> 00:42:19.219
That's right, all of you.

00:42:19.219 --> 00:42:24.045
[LAUGH] Okay, who's going to
speak up on how that class gives

00:42:24.045 --> 00:42:28.569
you the opportunity to show
up as more human or whole or

00:42:28.569 --> 00:42:32.705
authentic, lots of
the things Francisca said?

00:42:32.705 --> 00:42:33.776
&gt;&gt; I can jump in.

00:42:33.776 --> 00:42:38.251
I don't want to dominate the discussion,
but I'll say very quickly that similar to

00:42:38.251 --> 00:42:41.415
what Francisca said about
bringing all parts of yourself.

00:42:41.415 --> 00:42:45.189
That's something that if
you're any form of a minority,

00:42:45.189 --> 00:42:49.889
you may have experienced, knowing
that sometimes it can be difficult to

00:42:49.889 --> 00:42:52.752
bring all parts of your
identities to bear.

00:42:52.752 --> 00:42:55.304
And I think being in
interpersonal dynamics,

00:42:55.304 --> 00:42:59.361
you get to have difficult conversations
about how people perceive you and

00:42:59.361 --> 00:43:02.318
why that has to do with their
perception of identity.

00:43:02.318 --> 00:43:06.836
And also being able to call it and have
conversations about why some things that

00:43:06.836 --> 00:43:09.102
may have been said may be problematic.

00:43:09.102 --> 00:43:13.459
Or perhaps also bringing understanding
that there's an understanding of best

00:43:13.459 --> 00:43:17.697
intention, but you can still give feedback
about how things may land on you.

00:43:17.697 --> 00:43:20.530
And I think that makes you more
confident in owning who you are and

00:43:20.530 --> 00:43:22.541
walking into rooms
bringing your full self.

00:43:22.541 --> 00:43:26.796
So that definitely was critical for
me as part of the interpersonal dynamics.

00:43:26.796 --> 00:43:30.482
&gt;&gt; That's great, thank you.

00:43:30.482 --> 00:43:33.070
&gt;&gt; I might underline
something that Abby said.

00:43:33.070 --> 00:43:37.680
The story that Abby told from her LeAD
Labs experience was a lot of the lesson

00:43:37.680 --> 00:43:42.659
that I took from touchy-feely, which is
that sometimes we have feedback that we

00:43:42.659 --> 00:43:46.635
want to share and we approach it
as though we're building a case.

00:43:46.635 --> 00:43:50.683
And you're only sharing the feedback once
you're sure you have an airtight case, and

00:43:50.683 --> 00:43:53.633
that really means that you're
not sharing parts of yourself.

00:43:53.633 --> 00:43:58.119
And so sometimes what touchy-feely
also can push you is to provide

00:43:58.119 --> 00:44:03.084
feedback where you don't have evidence,
or there's a line that one of my

00:44:03.084 --> 00:44:07.997
facilitators said was, the feeling
often arrives before the thought.

00:44:07.997 --> 00:44:12.299
And you have a feeling, and then you
kind of, you do this very quickly,

00:44:12.299 --> 00:44:17.249
so you don't realize it, but you kind of
scramble to dress it up in fancy language,

00:44:17.249 --> 00:44:20.091
or you try and
give it intellectual clothing.

00:44:20.091 --> 00:44:24.653
But fundamentally, you just feel sad or
undervalued, or it's just something that

00:44:24.653 --> 00:44:28.972
you're almost embarrassed or ashamed
of because it's such a simple feeling.

00:44:28.972 --> 00:44:33.655
But naming those impressions, or
at least naming them to yourself,

00:44:33.655 --> 00:44:38.903
if not naming them, if you're saying,
hey, I'm feeling dismissed here,

00:44:38.903 --> 00:44:43.910
can often provide opportunities for
you to make yourself more known, but

00:44:43.910 --> 00:44:49.513
also to build a stronger relationship
with the people that you're working with.

00:44:49.513 --> 00:44:53.231
And as fluffy as that can sound,
that can allow you to build trust.

00:44:53.231 --> 00:44:56.672
And it doesn't mean that you're suddenly
revealing all parts of yourself, but

00:44:56.672 --> 00:45:00.063
you're incrementally building stronger
rapport with the people that you're

00:45:00.063 --> 00:45:01.190
working with regularly.

00:45:01.190 --> 00:45:05.352
So that was a really powerful learning for
me as well.

00:45:05.352 --> 00:45:07.500
&gt;&gt; Brian, is there a way,
I put you on the spot.

00:45:07.500 --> 00:45:10.366
I just realized some people might
not know how the class works.

00:45:10.366 --> 00:45:14.088
Is there a really short high level answer,
I know it's hard to describe, but for

00:45:14.088 --> 00:45:17.231
someone who's never heard about
interpersonal dynamics before?

00:45:17.231 --> 00:45:19.778
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think one way to
think about classes at the GSB

00:45:19.778 --> 00:45:22.949
is I've kind of been looking through
my notes since graduating and

00:45:22.949 --> 00:45:25.118
it's like there are three
types of classes.

00:45:25.118 --> 00:45:27.891
There are the ones where it's
like a new industry maybe and

00:45:27.891 --> 00:45:31.926
you're getting a glass bottom boat tour of
an industry that was unfamiliar for you.

00:45:31.926 --> 00:45:35.216
For me that was education,
the education industry.

00:45:35.216 --> 00:45:37.939
There are other ones where you're
learning new sets of tools and

00:45:37.939 --> 00:45:41.433
you're trying something out for the first
time and you're getting lots of reps.

00:45:41.433 --> 00:45:43.145
Like that negotiations class, or

00:45:43.145 --> 00:45:46.980
there's a corporate financial modeling
class where you learn to build an LBO

00:45:46.980 --> 00:45:50.003
model from scratch even if you
have never worked in finance.

00:45:50.003 --> 00:45:53.469
There's a third one where it's like,
and this is the last category,

00:45:53.469 --> 00:45:56.833
where it's like a new lens for
seeing and understanding the world.

00:45:56.833 --> 00:45:59.955
And I think touchy-feely kind of
straddles the second and the third one.

00:45:59.955 --> 00:46:02.865
You're learning new ways of
thinking about the world.

00:46:02.865 --> 00:46:05.086
You're looking at your interactions and

00:46:05.086 --> 00:46:10.044
you're kind of all of a sudden seeing them
diagrammed in your head of, what happened?

00:46:10.044 --> 00:46:13.635
This was an attempt to connect with me and
I missed it.

00:46:13.635 --> 00:46:17.066
And so now I feel like I'm going to
be more attentive to that.

00:46:17.066 --> 00:46:20.368
And then you're also having these
experiential practice groups.

00:46:20.368 --> 00:46:24.787
And so the way that's structured in
the class is you have lectures once a week

00:46:24.787 --> 00:46:29.483
where they walk through some kind of
content around like, I mentioned bins/s,

00:46:29.483 --> 00:46:32.884
this idea of what is the emotional
unit of communication?

00:46:32.884 --> 00:46:36.422
And the smallest building block of
that is something that's called a bin,

00:46:36.422 --> 00:46:39.467
which is defined as any attempt
to connect with another person.

00:46:39.467 --> 00:46:43.862
And might be telling a dad joke, or
smiling at someone when you see them, and

00:46:43.862 --> 00:46:47.794
it's just a question of,
does the person acknowledge that or not?

00:46:47.794 --> 00:46:50.856
And this is just a new vocabulary,
a new way of thinking about things.

00:46:50.856 --> 00:46:54.498
And then after these lecture classes,
you go into what's called a tea group,

00:46:54.498 --> 00:46:55.946
which is your learning group.

00:46:55.946 --> 00:47:01.051
It's 12 people, and you spend
something like seven hours in a room

00:47:01.051 --> 00:47:06.521
together without any clear agenda or
structure and just communicating.

00:47:06.521 --> 00:47:11.251
And a lot of what happens in there is
either providing feedback to people or

00:47:11.251 --> 00:47:15.218
sharing reactions to the ways
that people have landed on you,

00:47:15.218 --> 00:47:17.681
either in that session or in the past.

00:47:17.681 --> 00:47:21.924
And it's one of those hands-on
experiential learning courses where you

00:47:21.924 --> 00:47:26.238
really do push yourself to share feedback
that maybe is a little more direct

00:47:26.238 --> 00:47:28.087
than you're used to giving, or

00:47:28.087 --> 00:47:32.276
that reveals a little more of yourself
than you may be comfortable with.

00:47:32.276 --> 00:47:38.375
And those relationships
become very strong, actually.

00:47:38.375 --> 00:47:43.069
I'm working with someone who was in my
tea group when I was in touchy-feely

00:47:43.069 --> 00:47:47.690
because that relationship became like
a peer coaching relationship that

00:47:47.690 --> 00:47:52.553
continued past that class, and it was
a really formative experience for me.

00:47:52.553 --> 00:47:53.978
&gt;&gt; Awesome, that's great.

00:47:53.978 --> 00:47:56.557
Okay, I'm going to read another one,
this is from Dieter.

00:47:56.557 --> 00:47:59.893
Through these events, I've heard a lot
about professors being engaged outside

00:47:59.893 --> 00:48:03.389
the classroom with students' professional
aspirations and business endeavors.

00:48:03.389 --> 00:48:06.022
Does this hold true with
leadership development, or

00:48:06.022 --> 00:48:08.981
is that primarily done student
to student more or so?

00:48:08.981 --> 00:48:10.138
How are professors?

00:48:10.138 --> 00:48:13.937
Well, you could answer generally how
professors sort of empowered you along

00:48:13.937 --> 00:48:14.813
your two years and

00:48:14.813 --> 00:48:18.155
also specifically if they've
touched you in the leadership side.

00:48:18.155 --> 00:48:19.958
Anyway, Abby?

00:48:19.958 --> 00:48:20.938
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'm happy to take that.

00:48:20.938 --> 00:48:23.548
I mean, I think on a broad level
we've talked about I know people have

00:48:23.548 --> 00:48:26.310
mentioned different professors here
like Glenn Cravens, a great one.

00:48:26.310 --> 00:48:27.371
I still talk to him all the time.

00:48:27.371 --> 00:48:31.665
I work in marketing, so
he's a great person when writing by etc.

00:48:31.665 --> 00:48:36.109
I would say, I think one that fostering
relationships with Russia is the GSB is

00:48:36.109 --> 00:48:39.493
one of the I obviously knew coming
in that it was a good idea.

00:48:39.493 --> 00:48:42.138
But I don't necessarily know
that I realized how good

00:48:42.138 --> 00:48:45.151
an idea it was until I started
using them and leaning on them.

00:48:45.151 --> 00:48:47.886
I think I mentioned I've
already started working.

00:48:47.886 --> 00:48:52.339
I'm at a start up where I'm now managing
a team of 8 people now we just hired

00:48:52.339 --> 00:48:52.985
two more.

00:48:52.985 --> 00:48:57.110
And so, and it's my first experience in a
startup environment, I think for one, and

00:48:57.110 --> 00:49:01.082
when I've managed people before, it's
been in a much more structured setting.

00:49:01.082 --> 00:49:03.419
And so,
in terms of thinking about leadership and

00:49:03.419 --> 00:49:06.940
trying to one deploy the lessons of
leadership and thinking about my role.

00:49:06.940 --> 00:49:09.940
Is less of a like purely like hey,
so and so please do this and

00:49:09.940 --> 00:49:12.521
thinking about them as creative,
resourceful and

00:49:12.521 --> 00:49:15.956
whole human beings that I want to
help develop and make successful.

00:49:15.956 --> 00:49:20.361
And there's also quite frankly, like
leadership challenges that just come up

00:49:20.361 --> 00:49:23.655
right, like having I worked
during part of the GSB as well.

00:49:23.655 --> 00:49:26.999
But having people who are not
performing and having to kind of,

00:49:26.999 --> 00:49:29.978
have conversations that
are really tough around that.

00:49:29.978 --> 00:49:33.346
And having conversations when like,
I don't feel set up for

00:49:33.346 --> 00:49:36.714
success that it's much more
nuanced than it used to be like,

00:49:36.714 --> 00:49:40.035
I don't feel like I have the data
I need to fix this problem.

00:49:40.035 --> 00:49:43.148
It's much more structural across
the organization or things like that,

00:49:43.148 --> 00:49:45.820
because we're building a company
we're building a culture.

00:49:45.820 --> 00:49:50.073
And I can tell you that there are GSB
professors who I lean on quite heavily

00:49:50.073 --> 00:49:50.712
for this.

00:49:50.712 --> 00:49:53.744
So, a big one is
Ricky Frankel who is one of

00:49:53.744 --> 00:49:58.525
the she's one of the Arbuckle
Leadership Fellow Program leads.

00:49:58.525 --> 00:50:02.102
And she was my when I was a fellow
she was my clinic leader and

00:50:02.102 --> 00:50:05.395
kind of is just a huge,
huge mentor and coach of mine.

00:50:05.395 --> 00:50:08.893
And she's someone who I have role played
many of these conversations with both

00:50:08.893 --> 00:50:11.603
while I was at school and
actually since graduating school.

00:50:11.603 --> 00:50:14.867
Though it wasn't that long
ago not that far removed, but

00:50:14.867 --> 00:50:19.162
still having those conversations and
or respect another one who I have on.

00:50:19.162 --> 00:50:22.105
He teaches a course called conversations
and management, which is really around

00:50:22.105 --> 00:50:25.187
like, how do you think about the different
ways of having difficult conversations?

00:50:25.187 --> 00:50:26.790
What are the assumptions
that you're making?

00:50:26.790 --> 00:50:28.422
How do you think about them differently?

00:50:28.422 --> 00:50:31.996
And he's someone who I've had a lot of
correspondence with in serving this role

00:50:31.996 --> 00:50:33.982
as well of how do I think
about this problem?

00:50:33.982 --> 00:50:35.229
What are the things that I'm missing?

00:50:35.229 --> 00:50:36.472
Where are my blind spots?

00:50:36.472 --> 00:50:40.186
How should I actually like,
can we role play this conversation.

00:50:40.186 --> 00:50:43.892
And doing some of that I can tell you
it's like incredibly practical and

00:50:43.892 --> 00:50:46.934
very much applicable to the work
that I am doing right now.

00:50:46.934 --> 00:50:50.504
And so in some ways, I considered myself
very blessed because I have a reason to

00:50:50.504 --> 00:50:52.030
email those people all the time.

00:50:52.030 --> 00:50:56.682
But it's also it's really an important
resource that I leaned on a lot as

00:50:56.682 --> 00:51:01.578
I've kind of got my as I'm getting my
feet under me in the post school world.

00:51:01.578 --> 00:51:06.530
&gt;&gt; How about we wrap up but I'll go
right down the panel any tips to many

00:51:06.530 --> 00:51:11.225
we found out 9% of folks were
planning on applying this fall.

00:51:11.225 --> 00:51:15.205
So, any tips you might have as they're
facing that application process on things

00:51:15.205 --> 00:51:17.814
to think about if they're
thinking about Stanford.

00:51:17.814 --> 00:51:22.556
How about you, Brian?

00:51:22.556 --> 00:51:24.739
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, definitely apply.

00:51:24.739 --> 00:51:29.327
I think there's a lot of I think
being really clear to yourself and

00:51:29.327 --> 00:51:32.591
taking the time before
you write good essays.

00:51:32.591 --> 00:51:35.887
I think there's a lot of mechanical
stuff to focus on on the application and

00:51:35.887 --> 00:51:37.782
that's all things that you will get done.

00:51:37.782 --> 00:51:40.403
But giving yourself a little
bit of space to think about,

00:51:40.403 --> 00:51:42.921
what is it you really want to
get out of the experience.

00:51:42.921 --> 00:51:46.771
Because oftentimes people focus so
much on applying itself

00:51:46.771 --> 00:51:50.929
that there's just such a richness
of opportunity at Stanford,

00:51:50.929 --> 00:51:54.557
that you can't just figure
that out once you get there.

00:51:54.557 --> 00:51:57.329
And so knowing,
you don't need to have a really,

00:51:57.329 --> 00:52:01.091
deliberate clear plan of ABC,
more just saying, If nothing else,

00:52:01.091 --> 00:52:03.932
what do I want to get out of
these this year and half.

00:52:03.932 --> 00:52:07.176
And how do I want to change and what would
be something that I'd love to be able to

00:52:07.176 --> 00:52:09.006
say about myself at
the end of this journey.

00:52:09.006 --> 00:52:11.854
And just writing that down for yourself,
you don't have to share with anyone.

00:52:11.854 --> 00:52:15.420
But that can help you once you do get
there and you have this huge volume of

00:52:15.420 --> 00:52:18.461
things to choose from and
how you want to allocate your time.

00:52:18.461 --> 00:52:21.768
That can be something that you'd
revisit that's really helpful and

00:52:21.768 --> 00:52:25.190
that can often pull you through
the mechanical work of actually getting

00:52:25.190 --> 00:52:26.918
through the application process.

00:52:26.918 --> 00:52:28.064
So, good luck.

00:52:28.064 --> 00:52:31.152
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks Brian and Sarah.

00:52:31.152 --> 00:52:34.316
&gt;&gt; I was going to say something similar
to Brian and just really thinking for

00:52:34.316 --> 00:52:37.372
yourself what you want out of these
two years because it's an incredible

00:52:37.372 --> 00:52:38.083
opportunity.

00:52:38.083 --> 00:52:39.344
It's also the scary thing.

00:52:39.344 --> 00:52:42.622
I found a really intimidating to be like
what do I want out of these two years?

00:52:42.622 --> 00:52:43.941
It's a big daunting.

00:52:43.941 --> 00:52:47.353
So, I got some really good advice
to think about it as a hypothesis.

00:52:47.353 --> 00:52:50.182
So, come in with a number
of different hypotheses.

00:52:50.182 --> 00:52:51.583
And this is also how we run our startup.

00:52:51.583 --> 00:52:56.164
So, this is very analogous, but it's like
I have a the person I got this advice from

00:52:56.164 --> 00:52:59.514
had a hypothesis that he wanted
to be in the coffee industry.

00:52:59.514 --> 00:53:02.753
He wanted to work at a FinTech startup and
he wanted to like run a marathon.

00:53:02.753 --> 00:53:06.058
These are not related hypotheses,
but he systematically went out and

00:53:06.058 --> 00:53:07.626
did each of those three things and

00:53:07.626 --> 00:53:11.223
fell in love with this FinTech startup and
that's what he did after the GSB.

00:53:11.223 --> 00:53:13.479
And just taking an experimental
mindset to this and

00:53:13.479 --> 00:53:16.423
knowing that you do have time you
do have bandwidth to experiment.

00:53:16.423 --> 00:53:20.967
It's not all or nothing really for
me was a mindset change and

00:53:20.967 --> 00:53:23.385
how I approach the experience.

00:53:23.385 --> 00:53:26.128
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Carrie how about you?

00:53:26.128 --> 00:53:31.040
&gt;&gt; Well, first of all I want to reiterate
Brian's apply which with a shoot your shot

00:53:31.040 --> 00:53:35.014
because for myself I know I was
there are many times where I wasn't

00:53:35.014 --> 00:53:36.842
sure about getting into GSB.

00:53:36.842 --> 00:53:41.190
But you won't get in if you don't apply so
100% shoot your shot and

00:53:41.190 --> 00:53:45.108
try to get it I think in terms of advice,
I would say two things.

00:53:45.108 --> 00:53:48.774
One, have grace for yourself because
it's is a very rigorous process.

00:53:48.774 --> 00:53:51.510
And it can be daunting.

00:53:51.510 --> 00:53:55.869
There are many times where I had doubt,
but I think have grace and

00:53:55.869 --> 00:53:57.014
just keep at it.

00:53:57.014 --> 00:54:01.569
And then the second one is try to immerse
yourself into the community in any way

00:54:01.569 --> 00:54:02.142
you can.

00:54:02.142 --> 00:54:06.128
Whether that's attending programs like
this, it's reaching out to alumni current

00:54:06.128 --> 00:54:09.408
students, if you have any connections,
whether it's secondhand.

00:54:09.408 --> 00:54:13.807
Just try to try to learn as much as you
can about why the program is right for

00:54:13.807 --> 00:54:17.072
you because then it will
come through when you apply.

00:54:17.072 --> 00:54:20.978
&gt;&gt; Awesome, Abby, thank you.

00:54:20.978 --> 00:54:25.306
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'm trying not to reiterate all
of the the sage advice my peers here, and

00:54:25.306 --> 00:54:27.567
I don't know many things but one thing and

00:54:27.567 --> 00:54:31.260
I'm not the Dean of Admissions
either although she's in the room.

00:54:31.260 --> 00:54:37.373
But one thing that I do know is Stanford
doesn't want you because you're perfect.

00:54:37.373 --> 00:54:42.374
You're not going to be the greatest
leader ever because you're perfect.

00:54:42.374 --> 00:54:45.851
The people who you engage with and
meet with and who become close to you and

00:54:45.851 --> 00:54:47.166
all of these sorts things.

00:54:47.166 --> 00:54:49.157
It's not because you're perfect,

00:54:49.157 --> 00:54:53.596
it's because you have the interesting
quirks and flaws and personality traits.

00:54:53.596 --> 00:54:57.320
And things that make you and make you lead
in the authentic ways that are you and

00:54:57.320 --> 00:54:59.224
that help you engage wherever you are.

00:54:59.224 --> 00:55:02.878
Be at Stanford or be it at work wherever
it is the things that make you you.

00:55:02.878 --> 00:55:05.930
And so the goal is not perfection.

00:55:05.930 --> 00:55:09.041
I think the goal is to kind of
as people have talked about, and

00:55:09.041 --> 00:55:13.006
it's I think the start of a journey at
Stanford, right of starting to embrace

00:55:13.006 --> 00:55:16.921
those parts of yourself and knowing
that you can bring all of that together.

00:55:16.921 --> 00:55:17.772
And so the goal is so

00:55:17.772 --> 00:55:20.868
interesting when you're applying
to things like I must be perfect.

00:55:20.868 --> 00:55:24.442
And I think actually the reason that
everybody in the community is so amazing.

00:55:24.442 --> 00:55:28.589
And what really stands out to me
about it is that everyone wants

00:55:28.589 --> 00:55:32.910
an incredible leader because of
their quirks not despite them.

00:55:32.910 --> 00:55:34.116
&gt;&gt; Perfect, thank you.

00:55:34.116 --> 00:55:36.371
Francisca can you close this out.

00:55:36.371 --> 00:55:38.198
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think first,

00:55:38.198 --> 00:55:43.687
put in the work to be who you want to
be regardless of business school.

00:55:43.687 --> 00:55:47.463
So, business school is just
one step on the on the path.

00:55:47.463 --> 00:55:50.770
If it doesn't work out,
put yourself on that path,

00:55:50.770 --> 00:55:54.455
whether it's building habits,
getting to know people,

00:55:54.455 --> 00:55:58.530
looking ahead of things,
because this is not the end of the all.

00:55:58.530 --> 00:56:00.059
It's just one step on the way.

00:56:00.059 --> 00:56:03.267
And the second thing I would
say is stay off poets and

00:56:03.267 --> 00:56:08.043
quants and all those websites because
they're just going to stress you out and

00:56:08.043 --> 00:56:11.561
they don't really add that
much value to your journey.

00:56:11.561 --> 00:56:12.551
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:56:12.551 --> 00:56:15.404
Well thank all of this panelists for
coming here today.

00:56:15.404 --> 00:56:17.065
I've learned a ton and
really appreciate it.

00:56:17.065 --> 00:56:21.153
It's so wonderful to see all your
faces as well back together, and

00:56:21.153 --> 00:56:23.127
to all the prospects out there.

00:56:23.127 --> 00:56:24.720
We appreciate you coming.

00:56:24.720 --> 00:56:27.448
Hopefully, you listen to the sage advice,
shoot your shot.

00:56:27.448 --> 00:56:28.503
I'll steal from Carrie.

00:56:28.503 --> 00:56:32.238
And I hope to get a chance to read
your application this coming fall.

00:56:32.238 --> 00:56:33.458
Thanks so much.

00:56:33.458 --> 00:56:38.449
[MUSIC]

